# Force LF line endings on Linux-runtime artifacts so Windows clones
# with core.autocrlf=true don't break scripts deployed to the POC box.
# Mirrors what the wizard branch (feat/setup-wizard-walkthrough) does
# — keep both branches consistent.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
# systemd path units (WARP-843: droplet-openwrt-attach.path) parse on the
# appliance like .service files — same CRLF hazard, same pin.
*.path text eol=lf
# systemd timer units (WARP-2190: droplet-power-restore.timer) parse the same
# way — and a trailing CR lands INSIDE the value, so `Unit=foo.service\r`
# silently resolves to no such unit. Same hazard, same pin.
*.timer text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Extensionless host executables deployed to the box (e.g. /usr/local/sbin
# systemd ExecStart targets). No file extension, so the *.sh rule above
# doesn't catch them — pin explicitly so a Windows checkout never ships
# CRLF and breaks the shebang (203/EXEC) on the appliance.
scripts/host/usr-local-sbin/* text eol=lf

# Extensionless /etc/default tuning files. systemd's EnvironmentFile= keeps a
# trailing CR as part of the VALUE, so `FOO=1800\r` reaches the service as
# "1800\r" and every int() parse of it fails on a Windows-checked-out box.
scripts/host/etc-default/* text eol=lf

# Netplan drop-ins copied to /etc/netplan on the box. The *.yaml rule above does
# NOT match `70-eth.yaml.example` -- its final extension is `.example` -- so this
# file is otherwise unpinned, and a Windows checkout would hand an operator a
# CRLF netplan file to copy onto an appliance.
scripts/host/etc-netplan/* text eol=lf

# WARP-663 / ADR-020 — extensionless CLI dispatcher (sibling of droplet-admin).
# Same shebang-CRLF hazard as the host executables above; pin to LF so a
# Windows checkout never ships CRLF and breaks the shebang on the appliance.
scripts/droplet-image text eol=lf
# The release manifest + its schema are read by gen-manifest.py and the manifest
# is signed byte-for-byte; pin to LF so a Windows checkout doesn't alter the
# bytes the ECDSA signature covers. The public verify key likewise.
scripts/image/manifest.json text eol=lf
scripts/image/manifest.schema.json text eol=lf
scripts/image/keys/*.pub text eol=lf
# Autoinstall seed + GRUB menu are baked onto the ISO and parsed by cloud-init
# / GRUB on the appliance — CRLF would break both. Extensionless / .cfg, so the
# rules above don't catch them; pin explicitly.
scripts/image/autoinstall/* text eol=lf
scripts/image/grub-autoinstall.cfg text eol=lf

# Prisma migration SQL. file-edit-session.schema.test.ts (and the other
# *.schema.test.ts guards) read the migration bytes and comment-strip with
# `/--.*$/` before asserting on the DDL. `.` never matches `\r` and a
# non-multiline `$` only matches end-of-string, so a Windows checkout
# (core.autocrlf=true) that materializes migration.sql as CRLF leaves a
# trailing `\r` on every line — comment-stripping fails and a header line
# that legitimately NAMES an invalid idiom (`CREATE TYPE ... IF NOT EXISTS`)
# survives and trips the guard (WARP-1008). Every migration.sql is already
# committed as LF; pin so a Windows clone materializes it as LF too and the
# byte-level assertions hold on every platform.
apps/orchestrator/prisma/migrations/**/migration.sql text eol=lf

# WARP-1542 — the documented schema-drift baseline is line-compared against
# freshly-generated `prisma migrate diff` output (which is always LF). Same
# CRLF hazard as the migration SQL above; same pin.
apps/orchestrator/prisma/schema-drift-baseline.sql text eol=lf

# Anchor codegen artifacts. schemas/__tests__/codegen-drift.test.ts re-runs
# scripts/gen-anchor-schema.mjs and byte-compares its output against the
# checked-in TS + Python mirrors. The generator always emits LF (Node
# normalizes template-literal newlines to LF), and the Python mirror is
# already pinned by `*.py text eol=lf` above — but the generated TS had no
# pin, so a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) materialized it as CRLF
# and the byte-compare failed (LF regen vs CRLF on disk). Pin the whole
# anchor codegen triplet to LF so the comparison holds on every platform.
schemas/anchor.schema.json text eol=lf
scripts/gen-anchor-schema.mjs text eol=lf
packages/shared-types/src/anchor.ts text eol=lf

# WARP-1607 — Nextcloud config fragments bind-mounted into the container and
# parsed by PHP *inside* it (config/*.config.php and php conf.d/*.ini). Same
# Linux-runtime hazard as the scripts above, and the session ini's save_path is
# byte-sensitive, so pin them rather than rely on the host's autocrlf setting.
docker/nextcloud/* text eol=lf

# Vitest snapshots. The snapshot serializer builds its output in JS and
# always emits `\n`; vitest then byte-compares that against the `.snap` on
# disk. A Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) with no pin materializes
# every committed snapshot as CRLF, so the comparison is CRLF-on-disk vs
# LF-in-memory: the suite fails on snapshots nothing changed, and a `-u`
# run rewrites the whole file — which the clean filter converts straight
# back to LF on commit. Permanent phantom churn plus snapshot assertions
# that don't mean anything on Windows. Same class of bug as the migration
# SQL and anchor-codegen pins above; same fix (WARP-1567). Guarded by
# apps/orchestrator/src/__tests__/snapshot-eol.test.ts.
*.snap text eol=lf

# WARP-2192 — systemd/kernel config fragments deployed to the box. A trailing
# CR is NOT whitespace to either parser: modules-load.d would look for a module
# literally named "sp5100_tco\r" and find nothing, and systemd keeps the CR
# inside the value so `RuntimeWatchdogSec=120\r` fails to parse. Both failures
# are silent — the file reads correctly and the watchdog simply never arms.
scripts/host/etc-modules-load.d/* text eol=lf
scripts/host/etc-systemd-system.conf.d/* text eol=lf

# WARP-2574 - the host artefact manifest. Read by droplet-host-units audit from
# the box's checkout; a trailing CR would land inside a path and every lookup
# would miss. Same hazard as the units above, same pin.
scripts/host/MANIFEST text eol=lf

# WARP-2666 - the client-app release declaration, read field-by-field from the
# box's checkout by scripts/app-downloads/audit.sh. Extensionless, so nothing
# above catches it. A trailing CR lands in the LAST field of every row: on a
# 3-field `absent`/`blocked` row it ends up INSIDE the ticket, so `-\r` stops
# equalling `-` and the auditor refuses to give a verdict on a file that is
# perfectly correct. Same hazard as MANIFEST, same pin.
data/app-downloads/EXPECTED text eol=lf
